A||N||4||lews84||New Wheel Bearings||||5||
Z||000000||lews84||12-17-1999||02:02 PM||||Since I'm replacing the front rotors, I am also putting in new wheel bearing. I packed them like crazy but the new bearing are a lot stiffer than the old ones (85K miles). The old ones also have a lot of play in them. <P>Is this new stiffness normal and will it work  itself out quickly?<P>I am using Castrol Pyroplex Blue Super High Performance EP Grease.<P>Side note: I now see why theogre was so emphatic about cleaning the rotors. It seems impossible to keep them completely free of grease.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||12-17-1999||06:39 PM||||Yup,,,, you can never have them too clean before the pads go in.<P>are you sure you don't have them over torqued. That will make them stiff. They will be stiffer most times anyway: the new one aren't worn, and the grease is probly stiffer. <P>remember that there are 2 holes in the spindle. They are not square to each other, and line up diferantly with the castle to allow more presice adjusting. (same effect as rotating the retainer on fords.)<P>You do not want the center of the hub between the bearings full of grease. You'll blow the seal or dust cap out, or at least blow grease thru it or the dust cap. Alittle in there is ok but don't stuff it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||12-17-1999||06:56 PM||||Ogre,<P>UUUUUUMMMMM!  You said the "f" word!<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||12-17-1999||08:30 PM||||It's very easy to over tighten the hub nut like Ogre says. Use a torque wrench and spin the rotor by hand as you tighten them to about 12 ft/lbs (which ain't real tight). The rotor should still spin. Now back off the nut a little until you can get the cotter pin into one of the two holes. It's important to preload the bearings a bit and then back the nut off a little.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lews84||12-17-1999||10:11 PM||||Yep, torqued the nut to 12 ft/lb, per the manual. Assuming my 25 year old Craftsman automatic torque wrench still works and is calibrated that low. Then I backed the nut off until it was free and tightened it down by hand. The holes lined up almost perfectly first try. The rotor is still a little stiff, but there is no play.<P>I didn't know there were 2 sets of holes. I'll look for the other set when I do the other front wheel.<P>The new (shiny) rotor and caliper sure look pretty. To bad they won't stay that way.<P>I got everthing from the Fiero store and was suprised to find the races already installed in the front rotors. That saved some agrivation.<P>Thanks for all the responses to all my brake questions. <p>[This message has been edited by lews84 (edited 12-17-1999).]||||reg||10
